SBRK(2)                        P32W Unix                         SBRK(2)

NAME
       sbrk - change the data segment size

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       void *sbrk(int incr);

DESCRIPTION
       The break is the address just past the end of a process's
       data: exec(2) puts it at the end of the bss. sbrk moves it
       incr bytes up (or down, for a negative incr) and returns
       its old value, so sbrk(n) hands out n new bytes and sbrk(0)
       tells where the break is. Memory is mapped a 4 KiB page at a
       time; new pages read as zeros. Lowering the break unmaps
       whole pages above it.

       The heap grows up toward the stack, which grows down from
       0x800000 by up to 1 MiB; at least one unmapped page must
       stay between them. malloc(3) uses sbrk; a program that uses
       malloc should not move the break itself.

RETURN VALUE
       The old break; (void *)-1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOMEM  The new break would be below the end of the text or
               within a page of the stack, or there is no free
               memory for the new pages.

SEE ALSO
       malloc(3), exec(2), a.out(5)

BUGS
       There is no brk call to set the break to an address.
